.TH TUI_DECODER_INIT 3 2026-10-03 "libtuikit 0.1" "libtuikit Manual"
.SH NAME
tui_decoder_init, tui_decoder_free, tui_decoder_feed, tui_decoder_next,
tui_decoder_pending, tui_decoder_timeout, tui_event_name \- turn terminal
input bytes into key events
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <tuikit/event.h>
.PP
.BI "void   tui_decoder_init(tui_decoder *" d );
.BI "void   tui_decoder_free(tui_decoder *" d );
.BI "size_t tui_decoder_feed(tui_decoder *" d ", const char *" bytes ", size_t " n );
.BI "int    tui_decoder_next(tui_decoder *" d ", tui_event *" ev );
.BI "int    tui_decoder_pending(const tui_decoder *" d );
.BI "int    tui_decoder_timeout(tui_decoder *" d ", tui_event *" ev );
.BI "const char *tui_event_name(const tui_event *" ev ", char *" buf ", size_t " size );
.PP
.BI "TUI_EV_IS_CHAR(" ev ", " c )
.BI "TUI_EV_IS_CTRL(" ev ", " c )
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The decoder is a pure state machine: it reads no descriptor and keeps no
clock, so every sequence can be tested by feeding it bytes.
.BR tui_term_wait (3)
and
.BR tui_tty_read_key (3)
use it.
.PP
.BR tui_decoder_init ()
makes an empty decoder in caller-provided memory;
.BR tui_decoder_free ()
releases its paste buffer.
.BR tui_decoder_feed ()
queues bytes and returns how many it took: fewer than
.I n
when its queue (256 bytes) is full, in which case the caller takes events
with
.BR tui_decoder_next ()
and feeds the rest.
.BR tui_decoder_next ()
returns 1 and fills
.I ev
when a complete event is available, 0 otherwise.
.PP
.BR tui_decoder_pending ()
is non-zero when bytes are held for an incomplete sequence \(em a lone
ESC, half a UTF-8 character, an unfinished paste.
If no more input arrives within the caller's ESC delay, the caller calls
.BR tui_decoder_timeout (),
which resolves it: a lone ESC becomes
.BR TUI_KEY_ESC ,
"ESC [" and "ESC O" become Alt+[ and Alt+O, half a character becomes
U+FFFD, an unfinished paste is delivered as it is, and an unfinished
escape sequence is dropped.
.SS Events
.TP
.B TUI_KEY_CHAR
a printable character:
.I ev\->cp
is the code point and
.I ev\->utf8
its UTF-8 bytes.
Ctrl+letter is
.B TUI_KEY_CHAR
with the lower-case letter in
.I cp
and
.B TUI_MOD_CTRL
(Ctrl-C:
.BR TUI_EV_IS_CTRL(ev, \(aqc\(aq) );
a Ctrl or Alt chord has an empty
.IR utf8 ,
so inserting
.I utf8
into text is always safe.
.TP
.BR TUI_KEY_ENTER ", " TUI_KEY_TAB ", " TUI_KEY_BACKSPACE ", " TUI_KEY_ESC
CR and LF are Enter; DEL and BS are Backspace; Shift-Tab is
.B TUI_KEY_TAB
with
.BR TUI_MOD_SHIFT .
.TP
.BR TUI_KEY_UP " .. " TUI_KEY_F12
cursor, editing and function keys, with
.BR TUI_MOD_SHIFT ,
.B TUI_MOD_ALT
and
.B TUI_MOD_CTRL
in
.IR ev\->mods .
.SS Sequences understood
Following xterm's "Control Sequences":
.IP \(bu 2
CSI keys:
.BR "CSI A" \(en D ,
.BR "CSI H" ", " "CSI F" ,
.BR "CSI P" \(en S
(F1\(enF4),
.B CSI Z
(Shift-Tab), and
.BI "CSI " n " ~"
for Home 1/7, Insert 2, Delete 3, End 4/8, PgUp 5, PgDn 6, F5\(enF12
15/17\(en21/23/24.
The modifier parameter
.BI "CSI 1 ; " m " A"
gives Shift (1), Alt (2), Ctrl (4) and Meta (8, reported as Alt), plus one.
.IP \(bu
SS3 keys, sent in application cursor mode and for F1\(enF4:
.BR "ESC O A" \(en D ,
.BR H ,
.BR F ,
.BR P \(en S ,
and
.B ESC O M
(keypad Enter).
.IP \(bu
.BI "CSI " code " ; " m " u"
(the fixterms form).
.IP \(bu
ESC followed by a key is that key with Alt; two ESCs are two ESC keys.
.IP \(bu
UTF-8, with U+FFFD for malformed input; encoded C1 controls are dropped.
.IP \(bu
Bracketed paste,
.B CSI 200 ~
to
.BR "CSI 201 ~" ,
as one
.B TUI_EV_PASTE
of at most 1 MiB; the text in between is not interpreted.
.PP
A CSI sequence is parsed by its general form (parameter bytes, then
intermediate bytes, then one final byte), so one that is not a key \(em a
cursor position report, device attributes, mouse or focus reports \(em is
consumed whole and dropped, and never leaks into the input as typed
characters.
A sequence longer than 64 bytes is dropped the same way.
The same bytes decode to the same events however they are split across
reads.
.PP
.BR tui_event_name ()
writes a short English name of the event into
.I buf
and returns it: "a", "Ctrl+C", "Alt+x", "Ctrl+Shift+Left", "F5", "Resize
24x80", "Paste 12 bytes", "Hangup".
A control character is shown as U+00B7.
.SH RETURN VALUE
See above;
.BR tui_decoder_timeout ()
returns 1 with an event, 0 when nothing was pending or the pending bytes
were dropped.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tui_term_wait (3),
.BR tui_tty_read_key (3),
.BR libtuikit (7)
